{
  "rationale": "Evidence from iteration 9 (score 839.6, down from 859.0 at iteration 8). Raising base_speed from 1.1 to 1.25 turned slalom timeouts into finishes (7 timeouts to 2) but added off-track exits (1 to 7). Finished slalom runs fell from 12/20 to 11/20. Oval (20/20, 2.10 s) and s-bend (20/20, 2.62 s) held, but s-bend control effort rose to 9.5 and line-loss events were about 1 per run on the oval and s-bend. Speed is therefore helping the time budget but hurting lateral control on the slalom, so the optimum sits between 1.1 and 1.25. Change: base_speed from 1.25 to 1.17, roughly a 6% rise over iteration 8. Everything else stays as in iteration 8 (sensors at y=0.08, x=-0.04 to +0.04 at 0.02 spacing, size 0.016, wheel_track 0.11, mass 0.5, kp 2.0, ki 0, kd 1.8, last_direction line loss), so the effect of speed is isolated. Expected result: slalom finishes around 13 to 15 of 20, with mean slalom time under 2.9 s and fewer off-track exits than iteration 9, and oval and s-bend still near 20/20. Hypotheses: H1, slalom timeouts at 1.1 are caused by average speed, and off-track exits at 1.25 come from overshoot on the tight alternations, so a mid value should beat both. H2, line-loss events stay near 1 per run on the oval and s-bend because the tape exits the sensor footprint at higher speed; a slightly higher kd or sensor footprint would address this later. Next measurements: slalom finished count, ended_by split (timeout versus off_track), completion_time, and s-bend max_error and control_effort to check whether speed is still driving overshoot.",
